\: Macros common to the pluk documents (plan/docstep1.txt). Imported after pud/man.zmm.
\:
\:  \plukbegin{keys} \plukend  the document's pud::man environment (item 9)
\:  \plukdoc{name}{title}     opening of a document: roff NAME and DESCRIPTION, HTML h1
\:  \docsec{id}{caption}      a numbered section; \secnum{id} gives its number
\:  \sref{id}                 'section N', linked to the section (item 9)
\:  \page{name}{title}        HTML: continue on the page name.html (item 9)
\:  \console{..}              a console example (decision 3; see below)
\:  \file{name}               a data file beside the document (decision 4)
\:  \plan{name}               a file in the repository's plan/ directory (decision 4)
\:  \pluk_version             the version, from the stamp file (decision 9)

\: __o__o__o__O  version

\: out/stamp.version is written by the Makefile from pluk/Cargo.toml.

\'setx{pluk_version}{\'tr{{delete}{[:space:]}}{\'zinsert{stamp.version}}}

\'set{pluk_repo}{https://github.com/micans/pluk}

\: __o__o__o__O  document opening

\: The man page needs NAME (for whatis/apropos) and a heading for the text before the
\: first numbered section; the web page has neither, only the title. NAME and
\: DESCRIPTION are written directly, not through \sec, so that the section counter of
\: pud::man counts the numbered sections only (\secnum).

\: .nh: no automatic hyphenation, so that code (\v) is never hyphenated; groff's \%
\: would do it per word, but only at the start of a word: in '(\%--sam' it marks a
\: hyphenation point instead.

\: .ZR: a ruler above and below each console block (docstep1 item 5), redefining the
\: empty hook of base.zmm's verbatim. The line spans the room left of the current
\: indent; it is drawn with U+2500 (box drawing light horizontal) on the utf8 device,
\: with \- elsewhere (ascii, latin1; a plain - in \l draws nothing there). The rulers
\: are lines of their own, so a command copied from the block is unaffected.

\'def{plukdoc#2}{
   \'${roff}{
      \@{\N.nh\N}
      \@{\N.ie "\\*[.T]"utf8" .ds ZL \\[u2500]\N.el .ds ZL \\-\N}
      \@{\N.de ZR\N\\l'\\\\n(.lu-\\\\n(.iu\\\\*(ZL'\N..\N}
      \@{\N.SH NAME\N}\1 \@{\\-} \2
      \@{\N.SH DESCRIPTION\N}
   }
   \: html: the table of contents after the title (item 8); pluk.css places it beside
   \: the text on wide screens. The pages set {tocnum}{0}: \docsec captions carry
   \: their number.
   \'${html}{
      \'setx{_pluk_first}{\__fnout__}
      \'setx{_pluk_title}{\2}
      \@{\P<h1>}\2\@{</h1>\N}
      \"pud::man::maketoc"
   }
   \'${md}{
      \@{\P# }\2\@{\P}
   }
}

\: __o__o__o__O  pages (item 9)

\: \plukbegin{keys} .. \plukend: \begin{pud::man}{keys} .. \end{pud::man}, keeping
\: the keys for \page.
\:
\: \page{name}{title}: in HTML, the rest of the document goes to the page name.html,
\: titled 'title - <html_title>'. In roff and Markdown nothing: the man page and the
\: .md stay one document. Links to sections find their page in the .zmr
\: (pud::man::href).
\:
\: zoem keeps the environments per output file (each sink has its own dictionary
\: stack, src/sink.c), so pud::man cannot span a \writeto: \page ends it in the current
\: file (closing the page), \writeto's the next and begins it there again with the
\: same keys and the page's title (opening the page). Hence \page must not be used
\: inside another environment (a list), which would not continue on the new page.
\: The new page has a link to the first (the document title) and the table of
\: contents.

\: Every pluk man page asks for tbl ('\" t): harmless without tables (item 10).

\'def{plukbegin#1}{
   \'set{_pluk_keys}{{preprocess}{t}\1}
   \begin{pud::man}{\_pluk_keys}
}

\'def{plukend}{\end{pud::man}}

\'def{page#2}{
   \'${html}{
      \'setx{_pluk_pagetitle}{\2 \- \get{pud::man}{$html_title}}
      \end{pud::man}
      \'writeto{\1.html}
      \begin{pud::man}{\_pluk_keys {html_title}{\_pluk_pagetitle}}
      \@{\N<p class="docup">}\lref{\_pluk_first}{\_pluk_title}\@{</p>\N}
      \"pud::man::maketoc"
   }
}

\: \sref{id}: 'section N', linked to the section wherever it is (page, anchor). An
\: unknown id is reported by \ref; tools/runzoem.sh makes that an error (decision 5).

\'def{sref#1}{
   \'switch{\__device__}{
      {html}{\<a class="intern" href="\"pud::man::href"{\1}">{section \ref{\1}{num}}}
      {md}{\iref{\1}{section \ref{\1}{num}}}
      {section \ref{\1}{num}}
   }
}

\: __o__o__o__O  sections

\'def{docsec#2}{
   \'setx{_docsec_num}{\'let{\ctrput{pud::man::sec}+1}}
   \sec{}{\1}{\_docsec_num. \2}
}

\'def{secnum#1}{\ref{\1}{num}}

\: __o__o__o__O  console examples

\: \console{
\: $ pluk ...
\: expected output
\: }
\:
\: The block opens and closes on lines of their own, so that tools/ can find it (the
\: update step, item 7). Its text is literal except for zoem's own escapes: \\ \{ \}.
\: The newline after the opening brace is removed (the regex below is a literal
\: newline: zoem's tilde escapes have none for it). A leading newline would otherwise be
\: a blank first line, in roff (no-fill mode) and in HTML (white-space: pre).

\'if{\'cmp{eq}{\__device__}{md}}{}{
\'def{console#1}{
   \verbatim{\inspect{{mods}{posix,match-once}}{^
}{}{\1}}
}
}

\: Markdown: a ```console fence (decision 3; what doccheck.py runs). Here the newline
\: after the opening brace is kept and the one before the closing brace removed: with
\: white space not managed (\w), zoem prints a newline from the text followed by the
\: indent (\I, inside a list item), but not after a newline of its own (\N); so the
\: kept newline indents the first line, and \W\N gives the closing fence its indent once.
\: The regex is a literal newline followed by $.

\'${md}{
\'def{console#1}{
   \@{\P```console\w\+{3}}\inspect{{mods}{posix,match-once}}{
$}{}{\1}\@{\+{1}\W\N```\P}
}
}

\: __o__o__o__O  links

\: A data file: a relative link on the web (the data files are shipped beside the
\: pages, decision 8), the file name in roff.

\'${html}{\'def{file#1}{\lref{\1}{\v{\1}}}}
\'${roff}{\'def{file#1}{\v{\1}}}
\'${md}{\'def{file#1}{\lref{\1}{\1}}}

\: A plan file: plan/ is not part of the site, so an absolute link to the repository.
\: In Markdown too: the generated .md is checked in out/, where a relative link to
\: ../plan/ would not resolve, and the link then works wherever the .md is read.

\'${html}{\'def{plan#1}{\aref{\pluk_repo/blob/main/plan/\1}{\v{plan/\1}}}}
\'${roff}{\'def{plan#1}{\v{plan/\1}}}
\'${md}{\'def{plan#1}{\aref{\pluk_repo/blob/main/plan/\1}{plan/\1}}}
